WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Vsauce! Kevin here -- and when Sammy&nbsp;
“The Bull” Gravano ratted on the Mafia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.080
he wasn’t just saving his own skin --&nbsp;
he was engaging in a high-stakes game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.060
theory exercise… because we all&nbsp;
know that Snitches Get Stitches.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:21.180
Snitching is such a severe social transgression&nbsp;
that Judas Iscariot has been paying for snitching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:27.960
on Jesus to the Romans for nearly 2,000 years&nbsp;
-- and the 30 pieces of silver Judas received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.600
for his betrayal is still the benchmark for&nbsp;
an informant’s dishonorable compensation.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.840
Even little kids have a sense that snitching&nbsp;
is disgraceful conduct -- they ostracize the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.960
kids who “tattle” or “squeal.” Nobody likes a&nbsp;
tattletale! In Southern Italy, the code of conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:52.380
known as “omerta” governed the mafia’s refusal to&nbsp;
cooperate with authorities against others within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:59.580
the organization -- it was a blood oath -- and a&nbsp;
pentito -- someone who broke omerta -- was a rat.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.800
You take that oath, you never betray. You never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.200
talk. You never say anything.&nbsp;
No matter who violates you.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:11.220
So are Julian Assange and Edward Snowden heroic&nbsp;
whistleblowers who risked everything to lay bare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:18.960
the truth, or are they just weasels, stool&nbsp;
pigeons or traitors? It depends who you ask.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.500
But I think that what seems like an issue of&nbsp;
virtue and self-interest is a lot more complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:33.480
-- it’s a mix of human behavior and game theory,&nbsp;
a combination of morals and math… and how we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:40.200
actually play the game is so perverse that we make&nbsp;
it nearly impossible to actually get to the truth.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.500
WHAT’S A STOOL PIGEON? An antiquated&nbsp;
method for catching pigeons was to tie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.300
one pigeon to a stool -- as it flapped&nbsp;
its wings hopelessly trying to escape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:00.720
that attracted other pigeons to its location&nbsp;
where they could be captured easily.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.600
So calling a pigeon a “rat with wings”&nbsp;
works on a couple different levels.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.720
The most pure example of snitching and its&nbsp;
consequences is exposed in the Prisoner’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.020
Dilemma. You probably already know&nbsp;
how it works, so I’ll keep it brief.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.480
2 men are arrested, and they’re separated.&nbsp;
If neither snitches on the other one, police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:28.260
can still convict each of them on a lesser charge&nbsp;
with a 2-year sentence. If one turns on the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:33.840
that testimony will allow a conviction on&nbsp;
a greater charge with a 10-year sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:40.020
and the snitch will go free. If both&nbsp;
betray each other, it’s 5 years each.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:47.700
The payoff matrix shows all the possible outcomes&nbsp;
of both A and B snitching or NOT snitching -- and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.640
it immediately raises questions about the best&nbsp;
strategy. If you’re A, should you stay silent and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.000
hope B stays silent, too? 2 years. Does it make&nbsp;
more sense to betray your friend, and hope you go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.020
free if he maintains silence -- even if it means&nbsp;
risking 5 years if the other guys talks, too?

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:13.140
If you snitch, your possible outcomes&nbsp;
are 5 years or 0 -- betrayal means you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.580
will avoid a 10 year sentence&nbsp;
no matter what. Remaining loyal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.840
and not talking gives you possible&nbsp;
outcomes of 10 years and 2 years.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.620
So what’s loyalty worth to you?

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:33.720
It’s irrational to put stock in loyalty, and the&nbsp;
dominant strategy for both players is to betray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:40.440
one another. That’s the big game theory takeaway&nbsp;
from the Prisoner’s Dilemma. It's such a seminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.420
concept that its offshoots and implications&nbsp;
on decision theory are referenced over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.200
62,000 times on Google Scholar, and the most&nbsp;
comprehensive analysis of all-things-Prison's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:57.900
Dilemma -- Stanford's Encyclopedia of&nbsp;
Philosophy -- is over 30,000 words.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:04.800
In spite of nearly 75 years of scholarship,&nbsp;
we've obfuscated and flat-out ignored the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:13.140
aspect that dictates how this game affects&nbsp;
real lives in real life: The house always wins.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.620
In the prisoner’s dilemma, players are&nbsp;
incentivized to betray. The worst case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:25.320
scenario for a player is he honors the code&nbsp;
and his friend betrays him. The BEST case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:31.140
is he betrays, his friend stays loyal,&nbsp;
and he avoids prison time -- but even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:38.460
in that freedom scenario, he’s branded a&nbsp;
snitch for life. He’ll face consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.460
in the community for ratting out the&nbsp;
other guy. Even winning is losing.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.040
But there is no outcome where the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:52.020
lose. They can only win. It’s&nbsp;
just a question of how much.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.960
The FBI itself says that “informants have&nbsp;
played major roles in the investigation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:04.740
prosecution of a wide variety of federal crimes”&nbsp;
since the inception of the bureau in 1908… and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.760
even with clear published guidelines&nbsp;
about how informant programs work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:16.920
the whole thing is a murky soup of threats and&nbsp;
payoffs. And whether it’s the FBI or NYPD or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.860
Soviet KGB’s system of sekretny sotrudnik,&nbsp;
it’s always worked pretty much the same way.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.600
The cops need information to prosecute&nbsp;
criminals. If they don’t get it through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.960
investigation, they need to get&nbsp;
it through testimony. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.740
witnesses are totally comfortable&nbsp;
telling what they know… but most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:39.240
the time they’re not. Because of the,&nbsp;
“Snitches get stitches” repercussion.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.600
And that’s when the game theory changes.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:49.860
