00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:53.700
The one running the game has two options:&nbsp;
threats and incentives. Or both. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.340
prisoner’s dilemma, both players are offered&nbsp;
potential, but not guaranteed immunity if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:04.920
give up the other guy. Co-defendants and people&nbsp;
arrested for other stuff are offered everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:11.100
from lighter charges and sentences to full&nbsp;
amnesty, while incarcerated informants can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:18.120
get simple prison perks or full release. Paid&nbsp;
informants basically have a job and a paycheck.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:23.460
Informants have to decide how they&nbsp;
want to play to avoid punishment or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:28.560
balance their payoffs with the potential&nbsp;
consequences of those payoffs -- while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.720
the gamemasters wait around to see just&nbsp;
how much they’ve won. Not a bad deal.

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:38.880
Maybe that’s fair. In the prisoner’s&nbsp;
dilemma, we know that both players are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.560
criminals -- that’s why the cops are&nbsp;
100% confident they can convict them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:49.740
on that lesser 2-year charge. There’s no&nbsp;
wrongfully-charged man in the dilemma, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:55.980
there’s no guy trying to improve his already grim&nbsp;
circumstances or looking for a handful of cash.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:08:00.600
But rewarding snitches is such a twisted game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:06.420
it almost got innocent people executed&nbsp;
for killing a man who wasn’t even dead.

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:13.500
In 1819, Jesse and Stephen Boorn were convicted&nbsp;
and sentenced to death in Vermont for killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:18.180
their brother-in-law Russell Colvin. There&nbsp;
wasn’t a ton of evidence -- but Jesse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:24.900
Boorn’s cellmate Silas Merrill said that Jesse&nbsp;
confessed to him in detail. Merrill testified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:29.700
against the brothers and was rewarded with&nbsp;
his own freedom from a forgery conviction.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:36.660
But just before Jesse and Stephen were to be&nbsp;
hanged, Colvin was found alive in New Jersey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:42.600
He wasn’t even dead, let alone murdered by&nbsp;
the Boorn brothers. It was a rare win for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:48.300
justice -- and the cops who always benefit?&nbsp;
Even they kinda won, since they were spared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:54.480
from the wrong of putting innocent men&nbsp;
to death. Yes, a forger went free. Ehhh.

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:02.160
Merrill’s dishonesty reveals one of the obvious&nbsp;
problems with informant culture: an incentive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.220
lie. You already have an inherent sense of the&nbsp;
problems with snitching -- but let’s codify it.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.580
When it comes to criminal&nbsp;
justice, three things matter:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.300
what’s known, when it’s known, and who knows it.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:19.560
Truth is not necessarily part of that equation.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:24.780
To start, informants are suspect&nbsp;
because they're usually implicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:30.600
in anti-social behavior already -- so&nbsp;
they're kinda difficult to trust. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.720
wouldn’t they just make things&nbsp;
up to hurt the people they hate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:40.080
and protect their own? And don’t all the benefits&nbsp;
feel a little like a reward for criminals?

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.780
But the problems with the snitching game are&nbsp;
only the beginning. Sometimes informants are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:51.480
forced to play a game within that game.
 
Witness intimidation happens when criminals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:57.480
use their own system of threats and payoffs to&nbsp;
influence witness testimony. If a witness is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:02.460
forced to play a game where the house always&nbsp;
wins, and you’ll face consequences for their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:11.100
testimony? Then you can’t win, either -- so all&nbsp;
you can do is wreck the game by silencing them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:18.060
If you’ve ever watched any mob movie you’ve&nbsp;
seen how the Mafia’s omerta code is enforced.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:23.820
But what makes for great entertainment,&nbsp;
has ruined real lives like Anthony Brown’s.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:32.100
Nearly half, half, of death row wrongful&nbsp;
convictions have been tied to unreliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:37.380
informants. Anthony Brown was sentenced&nbsp;
to death in Florida in 1983 for murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.380 --> 00:10:44.880
but the actual killer testified against him in&nbsp;
exchange for his own lenience. And it was the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:52.380
for Randall Adams -- he was convicted of killing a&nbsp;
police officer in 1977 while the informant who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.380 --> 00:11:00.000
the real killer received immunity from prosecution&nbsp;
in exchange for his testimony. NEAT TRICK.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.520
Wanna go down a rat rabbit hole? Google&nbsp;
“Randy Steidl” -- in Randy’s case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:12.480
a cop sacrificed his own job to expose the truth&nbsp;
about the snitch system that wrongfully convicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:20.460
him of murder. Randy spent 17 years in prison for&nbsp;
murders he didn’t commit, and although he won a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:25.920
multi-million dollar settlement from his town&nbsp;
and the state of Illinois, he wasn’t pardoned.

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:32.640
So… as a society, what can we do&nbsp;
to combat the perverse incentives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:40.260
of snitching? We at least need to understand&nbsp;
how information games are played in real life.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:46.020
History has morphed the Roman goddess&nbsp;
Justitia into the modern image of Lady&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:51.540
Justice -- she’s usually depicted as&nbsp;
holding a scale that balances the act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:58.200
with its consequences. She’s blindfolded,&nbsp;
which is meant to symbolize impartiality.

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:04.740
But nearly 500 years ago, Flemish painter&nbsp;
Pieter Bruegel portrayed a static,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:12.780
unmoving Lady Justice wearing a blindfold to&nbsp;
suggest that she was failing to see injustice.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:19.020
So which is it? Is she impartial or just ignorant?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:26.460
I don’t know. But I do know that as long&nbsp;
as her lips are free to play the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:32.520
game in crime and punishment -- snitching&nbsp;
and stitching -- it might not even matter.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.760
And as always, thanks for watching.

